.TH ZSCALERCTL 1 "" "zscalerctl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zscalerctl \- read-only Zscaler configuration query and sanitized dump tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zscalerctl
[\fIglobal flags\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zscalerctl
is a read-only command-line tool for authorized Zscaler administrators to
query configuration, inspect inventory, and produce sanitized dumps. It is
machine-first: stdout carries data, stderr carries diagnostics, exit codes are
stable, and output is allow-list projected and redacted before it is written.
It never mutates tenant state and never emits secret material in normal output.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " name"
Profile name for optional owner-only config files. Environment variables still
take precedence.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
Path to an optional owner-only YAML config file containing profiles and secret
references.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " auto|table|json|ndjson|pretty"
Output format. Defaults to \fBauto\fR, which renders the human-readable
\fBpretty\fR form when stdout is a terminal and \fBjson\fR otherwise (pipe,
redirect, or \fB\-\-output\fR file), so interactive use is readable while
pipelines stay machine-parseable. \fBtable\fR is the tab-separated form;
\fBpretty\fR is a bordered/aligned overlay of the same sanitized data.
\fBndjson\fR is newline-delimited JSON \(em one compact record per line \(em for
streaming/pipeline consumers; it applies to resource \fBlist\fR/\fBget\fR/\fBshow\fR
output only and is rejected by other commands. Collection completes before
records are emitted, including in ndjson mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " count"
Return at most this positive number of records from a resource list, using
JSON output only. The result is an object with records and pagination members;
without this flag the existing JSON array output is unchanged. Collection,
projection, redaction, and filtering complete before this output page is cut.
This flag bounds rendered output, not API requests or collection memory.
.TP
.BI \-\-offset " count"
Skip this nonnegative number of matching records before applying the limit.
Requires \fB\-\-limit\fR; defaults to zero. Each invocation recollects the
resource, so offsets do not provide a stable snapshot across calls. Pagination
metadata reports matched_count, returned_count, has_more, and next_offset;
collection_complete is true only after successful collection.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " path"
Create or replace a restricted regular file with command output
instead of writing to stdout (not valid with dump). This is a local filesystem
side effect; agents should use it only when that write is explicitly intended.
Publication uses a same-directory rename and is atomic on Unix. Windows does
not have an atomic replacement guarantee. On Windows, mode 0600 does not
restrict ACLs; use a destination directory with an appropriately restricted ACL.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Per-request timeout (e.g. 30s). Must be positive. This bounds each HTTP request
(including the token exchange), not the whole invocation; a large \fBdump\fR
making many requests is not capped end-to-end. Impose a whole-run limit
externally (for example with \fBtimeout(1)\fR) if you need one.
.TP
.BI \-\-from-dump " dir"
Read a complete saved dump with ordinary resource list/get/show commands and
\fBrefs\fR.
Bypasses configuration, credential providers, SDK construction, and network
access. Cannot be combined with --profile or --config. Uses the stored redaction
mode and rejects an explicitly different mode. Admission validates all saved
resources and is capped at 256 MiB of serialized resource data, not decoded
memory. Each invocation reloads the directory. Missing resources and IDs are
errors; present empty lists succeed. Narrowing flags do not reduce admission.
.TP
.BI \-\-redaction " standard|share|paranoid"
Redaction mode applied to rendered fields.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " auto|always|never"
Color policy for human output.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable color output (equivalent to \-\-color never; NO_COLOR is also honored).
.TP
.B \-\-no-cache
Bypass the API cache where supported.
.TP
.BI \-\-log-level " off|error|warn|info|debug"
Diagnostic logging to stderr. Defaults to off. Diagnostics are metadata only
and never include record values, credentials, or secrets. During a long
\fBdump\fR, \fB\-\-log-level info\fR reports progress on stderr: a starting
event with the selected resource count, one event per resource as it is read,
and a completion summary with the resource and error counts.
.TP
.BI \-\-fields " a,b,c"
Comma-separated list of output fields to keep, applied across all formats. It
can only narrow the already-sanitized output; it cannot reveal a dropped or
secret field. An unknown field name is a usage error before configuration or
credentials are loaded.
.TP
.BI \-\-filter " key=value | key~value"
Narrow list results to records whose rendered field value matches:
\fBkey=value\fR is an exact match, \fBkey~value\fR a case-insensitive
substring match. A key may be a dotted path ending at a nested leaf field,
such as \fBgroups.name\fR; it matches when any list element it reaches
matches, and a path ending at an object is a usage error. A top-level key
matches the whole rendered value, and a top-level list also matches when any
one element does. Repeatable; all filters must match
(AND), and each dotted filter may be satisfied by a different element. Valid for
\fBlist\fR operations only; anywhere else it is a usage error. Filtering runs
strictly after projection and redaction, so it can only narrow the sanitized
record set; a record lacking the key does not match. A filter naming a field
the active redaction mode omits, or a secret field, is a usage error (exit 2)
before any read, since it could never match. No matches is success (exit 0) with an
empty result. An unknown catalog field name is a usage error (exit 2) before
configuration or credentials are loaded; discover names with schema list.
.TP
.BI \-\-count\-by " a,b,c"
Count list records instead of listing them. Records are grouped by the
rendered values at each comma-separated path; a path such as
\fBgroups.id\fR counts per list element, once per record, so counting users by
\fBgroups.id\fR gives members per group. A record with no value at a path
counts under null, so the counts cover every record. Paths through the same
list pair values from the same element, so \fBgroups.id,groups.name\fR gives
each group its own name; paths on different branches combine. JSON output is
an object with \fBcount_by\fR, \fBrecords\fR (records counted),
\fBdistinct\fR (value combinations), \fBtruncated\fR and \fBcounts\fR (largest first), each
count \fB{"values": {...}, "count": N}\fR; NDJSON emits one count row per
line. Filters and search apply first; \fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR keeps the
\fIN\fR largest counts and sets \fBtruncated\fR when it dropped any. Valid for \fBlist\fR operations only; combining it with
\fB\-\-fields\fR or \fB\-\-offset\fR, an empty or repeated \fB\-\-count\-by\fR, repeating a path, naming an object rather than a leaf, or
naming a field the active mode omits is a usage error (exit 2) before any read.
.TP
.BI \-\-search " term"
Narrow list results to records where any rendered field value contains
\fIterm\fR (case-insensitive), including array and nested values via their
rendered string forms. Valid for \fBlist\fR operations only; same
post-projection guarantees as \fB\-\-filter\fR, and may be combined with it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B <product> <resource> list
List a resource for a product (\fBzia\fR, \fBzpa\fR, \fBztw\fR, \fBzcc\fR,
\fBzidentity\fR), e.g. \fBzscalerctl zia locations list\fR.
.TP
.B <product> <resource> get <id>
Get a single resource by id, where the resource supports it.
.TP
.B <product> <resource> show
Show a singleton/status resource that yields one record.
.TP
.B zia url-lookup <url> [url...]
Resolve the URL category classifications for one or more URLs or domains,
including security-alert categories and the matched cloud application. A
diagnostic query, not a catalog resource: the backing endpoint performs a pure
lookup and never modifies tenant state. At least one URL is required.
.TP
.B zia admin-audit-logs status
Show the status of the tenant's admin audit log report: a normalized status,
the progress count when supplied (including an explicit zero), the completion
time when known, and an allow-listed error code. A read-only diagnostic: it
performs one status operation, with HTTP retries following the shared SDK
policy. It never creates, downloads or cancels a report. The API's free-form
error message is never shown.
.TP
.B dump \-\-out <dir> [\-\-products ...] [\-\-resources ...] [\-\-continue-on-error] [\-\-force]
Write a sanitized multi-resource dump (manifest.json, redaction_report.json,
per-resource files, and errors.ndjson on failure) into a new directory.
\fB\-\-force\fR replaces an existing destination, but only after verifying it is
itself a zscalerctl dump directory; it refuses to follow symlinks and refuses
the current working directory, the home directory, and the filesystem root.
.TP
.B refs to <product> <resource> <id>
Read every resource with a field that points at <product> <resource> and list
the records whose field holds <id>, with each record's id and name. Fields the
redaction mode omits, resources without a list or show operation, and failed
reads are listed as not checked. It reports what references what; it never says
what a reference does. Works with \fB\-\-from-dump\fR.
.TP
.B refs check <product> <resource>
Read <product> <resource> and every resource its reference fields point at, and
list each referenced id that is not in its target lists as read. A field whose
target could not be read is listed as not checked and contributes nothing.
"Not found in what was read" is all it claims. Works with \fB\-\-from-dump\fR.
.TP
.B diff <old-dump-dir> <new-dump-dir> [\-\-products ...] [\-\-resources ...] [\-\-ignore-operational] [\-\-detail] [\-\-allow-partial] [\-\-fail-on-drift]
Compare two existing dump directories and emit a local drift report. List and
array fields are compared in order, so reordering a list is reported as drift.
The command performs no live API calls and does not schedule collection; use
cron, CI, or another external scheduler to create dumps at the cadence you need.
The flags are:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-detail
Add record-level rows (the individual added/removed/changed records) to the
\fBtable\fR and \fBpretty\fR reports. It is a no-op under \fB\-\-format json\fR,
whose report already contains the full record-level detail.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore-operational
Ignore fields classified as operational metadata (such as identifiers and SDK
timestamps) on keyed and singleton resources, so only configuration changes are
reported.
.TP
.B \-\-allow-partial
Permit the comparison to proceed when an input dump is partial (was written
with some resources failing) instead of refusing it.
.TP
.B \-\-fail-on-drift
Return exit 7 when added, removed, or changed records are detected; without it,
drift is reported with exit 0.
.RE
.TP
.B schema list
Print the resource catalog (products, resources, operations, field model).
.TP
.B schema describe <product> <resource>
Inspect config-free candidate field semantics for one resource. The pilot
reviews selected fields of ZIA locations, URL filtering rules, and rule labels.
Known resources outside the pilot report not_reviewed with empty fields.
Unknown resources exit 4. The explicit redaction mode filters described fields;
the default is standard, independent of configuration. JSON is recommended.
.TP
.B schema references <product> <resource>
List the catalog fields that point at one resource: each entry names the
resource and field, the \fB\-\-filter\fR path that holds the target's id
(\fBmatch\fR), the redaction modes that render it, and every resource the
field's ids may belong to (\fBtargets\fR). A reference no mode renders is
listed without a match path. Fields whose target is not verified are not
listed, so an empty list does not prove nothing refers to the resource.
Config-free; unknown resources exit 4.
.TP
.B schema gaps [product]
List the Zscaler surfaces zscalerctl does not read: each entry names the
\fBsurface\fR, a \fBstatus\fR (\fBdeferred\fR may be added once its blocker
clears; \fBout_of_scope\fR is not planned), a \fBreason\fR, a value-free
\fBnote\fR, and, for an alternate view or an action whose status is read, the
covered resources to read \fBinstead\fR. Asking to
read a declared gap, or a common alias such as \fBzpa access-policies\fR,
exits 4 with a message naming it. Config-free; an unknown product exits 2.
.TP
.B doctor
Report configuration readiness and the resolved auth mode without exposing
secrets. \fBdoctor\fR inspects \fBZSCALERCTL_*\fR configuration only and never
contacts Zscaler; its \fBlive_api\fR field is derived from whether credentials
are configured, not from a live reachability probe.
.TP
.B auth status
Report credential presence and live-API reachability.
.TP
.B config show
Render the active, secret-safe configuration.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish|powershell
Print a shell completion script.
.TP
.B introspect
Print a machine-readable JSON map of all commands, flags, resources, and exit
codes. Version 3 describes each command's possible effects (local filesystem,
network, and configuration-dependent provider process activity) as structured
\fBeffects\fR; the tenant itself is never changed (\fBread_only\fR). Config-free and
network-free; safe to run without credentials. Use
\fB\-\-format pretty\fR for a human-readable indented tree.
.TP
.B version
Print version, commit, and build date.
.TP
.B help
Print usage.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Credentials and settings are read only from the \fBZSCALERCTL_*\fR namespace;
the tool deliberately ignores the SDK's own environment variables.
.TP
.B ZSCALERCTL_CLIENT_ID, ZSCALERCTL_CLIENT_SECRET, ZSCALERCTL_VANITY_DOMAIN
OneAPI (Zidentity) credentials. \fBZSCALERCTL_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE\fR reads the
secret from an owner-only file.
.TP
.B ZSCALERCTL_ZPA_CUSTOMER_ID, ZSCALERCTL_ZPA_MICROTENANT_ID
Required ZPA customer id and optional microtenant id.
.TP
.B ZSCALERCTL_ZIA_USERNAME, ZSCALERCTL_ZIA_PASSWORD, ZSCALERCTL_ZIA_API_KEY, ZSCALERCTL_ZIA_CLOUD
Legacy ZIA credentials (with \fB_FILE\fR variants for password and API key)
when \fBZSCALERCTL_AUTH_MODE=zia-legacy\fR.
.TP
.B ZSCALERCTL_PROFILE
Default for \fB\-\-profile\fR. With neither set, the config file's
\fBdefault_profile\fR is used; when the file defines more than one profile,
a command that reads a tenant and succeeds prints a one-line stderr notice
naming it. A failing command's stderr is only its error envelope.
.TP
.B ZSCALERCTL_CONFIG
Default config file path for profile loading. Environment variables override
profile values.
.TP
.B ZSCALERCTL_REDACTION, ZSCALERCTL_NO_CACHE
Optional defaults mirroring the matching flags.
.TP
.B ZSCALERCTL_AUTH_MODE
Authentication mode: \fBoneapi\fR (the default) or \fBzia-legacy\fR. There is no
flag equivalent.
.TP
.B ZSCALERCTL_CLOUD
OneAPI gateway: \fBPRODUCTION\fR (the default), \fBBETA\fR, \fBGOV\fR or
\fBGOVUS\fR. Tenants on product clouds such as zscalertwo, zscalerthree or
ZPATWO use \fBPRODUCTION\fR. Environment-only; there is no flag equivalent.
.TP
.B ZSCALERCTL_PROXY_URL, ZSCALERCTL_PROXY_FROM_ENV
Explicit proxy settings; ambient HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY discovery is off by
default.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to any value, disables colored output (equivalent to \fB\-\-color
never\fR). Honored in addition to the \fBZSCALERCTL_*\fR namespace.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Internal error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an invalid CLI flag, an invalid resource id, an invalid
\fBZSCALERCTL_*\fR configuration value, or invalid proxy configuration.
.TP
.B 3
Missing or invalid credentials.
.TP
.B 4
Resource not found or unsupported, including a product or resource the tenant
is not entitled to.
.TP
.B 5
Live API access failure.
.TP
.B 6
Partial dump (some resources failed; see errors.ndjson).
.TP
.B 7
Drift detected when \fBdiff \-\-fail-on-drift\fR is used.
.SH EXAMPLES
Enumerate every product, resource, operation, and field in the catalog:
.RS
.EX
zscalerctl \-\-format json schema list
.EE
.RE
.PP
List a resource as machine-readable JSON:
.RS
.EX
zscalerctl \-\-format json zia locations list
.EE
.RE
.PP
Get a single resource by id:
.RS
.EX
zscalerctl \-\-format json zia locations get 12345
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show a singleton/status resource:
.RS
.EX
zscalerctl \-\-format json zia advanced-settings show
.EE
.RE
.PP
Narrow a list with a case-insensitive substring filter:
.RS
.EX
zscalerctl zia url-filtering-rules list \-\-filter name~social
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a sanitized, single-product dump to a scratch directory:
.RS
.EX
zscalerctl dump \-\-products zia \-\-out ./scratch-live-dump
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compare two existing dumps and fail when drift is detected:
.RS
.EX
zscalerctl \-\-format json diff ./old-dump ./new-dump \-\-fail-on-drift
.EE
.RE
.PP
Resolve the URL category classification for a URL:
.RS
.EX
zscalerctl zia url-lookup https://example.com
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
Project documentation under \fBdocs/\fR, including ARCHITECTURE.md,
THREAT_MODEL.md, and the published dump and diff schemas in docs/schema/.
